  "response": "The dispute over whether a model's benchmark score reflects genuine generalization or the recall of contaminated training text cannot be settled by further inspection of performance on the contested benchmark itself, since any score on that benchmark is compatible with either reading. It is settled instead by measurements that the training corpus could not have anticipated, of which two kinds carry most of the argumentative weight: newly authored test material, and direct audits of what the pretraining data actually contained.\n\nGSM8K is a benchmark of grade school math word problems released by OpenAI. Scale AI researchers constructed GSM1k as a fresh benchmark matched to GSM8K's difficulty and distribution. Human annotators authored the GSM1k problems after GSM8K was published. The GSM8K training split contains 85,000 problems. Building GSM1k resulted in a workable estimation procedure: researchers estimate overfitting by comparing a model's accuracy on an original benchmark with its accuracy on a distribution-matched replica.\n\nThe Scale AI study reported accuracy drops of up to 13 percent on GSM1k for some model families. A large accuracy decline from the original benchmark to its held-out replica indicates reliance on memorized items; that is, scoring much worse on the fresh replica than on the original benchmark shows that the model had partly memorized the original. Read against that standard, the double-digit drops recorded on GSM1k are evidence that benchmark test items were included in the model's pretraining corpus. Paraphrased versions of the benchmark items circulated in web pages that were scraped for training, and at least one of these two conduits, the verbatim test items in the corpus or the scraped paraphrases, carried the leakage, perhaps both. N-gram overlap searches identify verbatim benchmark text inside pretraining corpora, and a positive match of that kind confirms that the test items sat in the training data; canary GUID strings, by contrast, prevent web crawlers from copying benchmark data into training corpora.\n\nA further line of attack holds a problem's surface structure fixed and varies only what fills it. The GSM-Symbolic benchmark generates problem variants from symbolic templates by substituting names and numerical values, and this generation procedure led directly to the result of Mirzadeh and colleagues, who reported that model accuracy declined when the numerical values in problems were altered. Where accuracy moves with substitutions that leave the required reasoning entirely untouched, the generalization reading cannot be sustained, and the contamination reading is what the evidence leaves standing.",
